docs {
  # POST /v1/admin/notifications/register

  Register an operator companion-app device to receive end-to-end-encrypted operational
  alerts. The admin analog of `POST /v1/notifications/register`.

  **This route requires a device-signed request** — the master token is refused with 400. That
  is the security property, not a limitation: the registration is keyed by the device the
  credential authenticates, so a device can register a push key for itself and for nothing
  else. A credential that authenticates no particular device (the master token) could otherwise
  redirect another device's alerts.

  Sign the canonical envelope as for every admin device request:
  `METHOD \n PATH \n TIMESTAMP \n NONCE \n sha256_hex(BODY)`, and send the
  `X-Admin-Device` / `X-Admin-Timestamp` / `X-Admin-Nonce` / `X-Admin-Signature` headers.

  ## Request

  - `notificationPublicKey` — this device's P-256 `did:key`. One notification keypair per
    device, shared across every relay it is paired with.
  - `apnsToken` — the APNs device token (hex).
  - `apnsTopic` — the companion app's bundle id.
  - `ping` (optional, default `false`) — metadata-minimizing ping mode: content-free
    `content-available: 1` background pushes instead of sealed payloads, as on the account
    surface. iOS aggressively throttles background pushes, so alert quality suffers.

  ## Response (200)

  ```json
  { "status": "pending" }
  ```

  The relay handle is minted in the background, as on the account surface.

  Revoking the device (`POST /v1/admin/devices/:id/revoke`) stops its alerts immediately — the
  fan-out query joins on the revocation tombstone — and drops this registration and its relay
  handle.

  ## Errors

  - `400` — the master token was used instead of a device signature, or a malformed field
  - `401` / `403` — invalid signature, replayed nonce, or a revoked device
  - `501` — this instance has no `[notifications] relay` configured
}
